 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Draw a tangent to the curve at the required point",
   "Pick two points on the tangent line far apart for accuracy",
   "Calculate the gradient: rise ÷ run",
   "For iteration: substitute x₀ into the formula to get x₁, then x₁ to get x₂, etc.",
   "Continue until the required number of decimal places are stable"
  ],
  "title": "How to Find Rates of Change & Use Iteration",
  "content": "<p>The <strong>rate of change</strong> is the gradient of a graph at a point. For a curve, draw a <strong>tangent</strong> (straight line touching the curve) at the point and find its gradient using \\(\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\\).</p><p><strong>Iterative processes</strong> use a formula like \\(x_{n+1} = f(x_n)\\) to generate a sequence that converges to a solution. Start with an initial value \\(x_0\\), substitute to find \\(x_1\\), then \\(x_2\\), and so on until the values stabilise to the required decimal places.</p><p>For estimation of area under a curve, use the <strong>trapezium rule</strong>: divide the area into strips and sum the trapeziums.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Use the iterative formula \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt{5x_n + 3}\\) with \\(x_0 = 4\\) to find \\(x_1\\), \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\) to 4 d.p.</strong></p><p>\\(x_1 = \\sqrt{5(4) + 3} = \\sqrt{23} = 4.7958\\)</p><p>\\(x_2 = \\sqrt{5(4.7958) + 3} = \\sqrt{26.979} = 5.1942\\)</p><p>\\(x_3 = \\sqrt{5(5.1942) + 3} = \\sqrt{28.971} = 5.3825\\)</p>"
 },
